# Auto detect text files and perform LF normalization
* text=auto

# ─── LF pins (#658 Tier 1, item 10) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `text=auto` above stores everything as LF in the repo and checks it out in the
# platform's native ending — which on a Windows working tree means CRLF. That is
# harmless for Rust, WGSL and JSON — and for *most* Markdown — and actively breaks the
# files below. ⚠️ Markdown was listed as harmless without qualification until 2026-08-13,
# when a `SKILL.md` proved otherwise; see the SKILL.md pin at the foot of this file.
# Each pin exists for a specific failure, not for tidiness:

# Shebangs. A `#!/usr/bin/env bash` line ending in CRLF makes an interpreter lookup
# search for the program `bash\r`, which does not exist; the error is
# `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env^M: no such file or directory`, which reads like a
# missing tool rather than a line-ending bug.
#
# ⚠️ **MEASURED CORRECTION (organon#658, the RTX 5090 workstation, 2026-08-07).** This
# pin originally justified itself with that failure happening *on the Windows box*, and
# **it does not.** The CRLF is real there — `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r\n` confirmed at byte
# level, `core.autocrlf=true` system-wide — but that box's shell is MSYS2/MINGW64 bash
# 5.3.15, which **strips the CR** from both shebangs and `[ "$V" = "x" ]` comparisons.
# `./verify.sh --help` and `./deploy.sh --help` both run correctly and exit 0. Git Bash
# is not the victim this pin was written for.
#
# The pin is kept anyway, on narrower and honest grounds:
#   * **WSL and any non-MSYS bash** do NOT strip the CR — a checkout shared with WSL, or
#     a script run under a plain Linux bash on a mounted Windows tree, hits the real
#     failure. MSYS's leniency is a property of one shell, not of the platform.
#   * **Editor and tooling churn.** A CRLF working copy invites an editor or formatter to
#     write CRLF back into files the repo stores as LF, producing whole-file diffs on
#     untouched scripts.
#   * It is free, and it removes a variable rather than relying on a lenient shell.
#
# Affected: `native/verify.sh`, `native/bundle.sh`, `native/deploy.sh`, the ten
# `.claude/hooks/*.sh`, `scripts/export-public.sh`, `web/devloop.sh`.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same mechanism, different interpreter — and the same correction applies, since these
# are launched from the same shell: `native/tools/check-world-bindings.py` and
# `check-editor-extract.py` are mode 755 and run as `./check-….py`. Python source itself
# is newline-agnostic, so this pin is about the shebang only, and it is kept for the WSL
# / non-MSYS / editor-churn reasons above rather than for a Git Bash failure.
*.py text eol=lf

# ─── .ps1 is deliberately NOT pinned (#658 Tier 3) ───────────────────────────────────
#
# `native/bundle.ps1` and `native/deploy.ps1` are the Windows deploy pipeline. Every pin
# above exists to stop a CRLF checkout breaking a file that a Unix tool reads; these two
# are the exact inverse — they only ever run on Windows, under a parser that accepts LF
# and CRLF identically, and CRLF is the native convention for the editors that will open
# them there. So `text=auto` (LF in the repo, CRLF in a Windows working tree) is already
# the right answer and a pin would be cargo-culting the shebang rule onto a file with no
# shebang.
#
# The one case that WOULD need a pin is Authenticode-signed scripts — a signature block
# covers the literal bytes, so line-ending translation invalidates it. Nothing here is
# signed (see deploy.ps1's header on why Windows needs no signing step). If that ever
# changes, this is the note that says to add `*.ps1 text eol=crlf` and why.
#
# 🚨 ENCODING IS A SEPARATE QUESTION FROM LINE ENDINGS, and only the second one is settled
# above. The paragraph about "a parser that accepts LF and CRLF identically" is true and
# says nothing about how that parser decides what the BYTES mean. **Windows PowerShell 5.1
# reads a BOM-less .ps1 as ANSI/CP1252**, and 5.1 is the only PowerShell on a stock Windows
# install — `pwsh` is a separate download. Both of these files carry non-ASCII prose, so
# without a BOM neither could be parsed at all on the machine they exist to serve: measured
# 2026-08-20, 8 parse errors in deploy.ps1 and 2 in bundle.ps1, zero with a BOM prepended.
#
# So both files are UTF-8 **with a BOM**, deliberately, and that is not a `.gitattributes`
# rule — a BOM is content, and git stores it like any other byte. It is enforced in CI
# instead (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, "Validate the PowerShell deploy scripts"): a .ps1
# must be pure ASCII or carry a BOM. ⚠️ Do NOT add `working-tree-encoding` here to express
# this. That attribute makes git re-encode on checkout, which changes the bytes a signature
# would cover and would re-open the question the paragraph above just closed.

# The frame harness's scene files. `verify.sh` pulls directives out of them with
# `sed -n "s/^#![[:space:]]*<key>[[:space:]]\{1,\}\(.*\)$/\1/p"`, and `.*` happily
# captures a trailing CR — so `#!settle 1200` yields the value `1200\r` (breaking the
# arithmetic) and `#!checks nonblack,animates` yields the check name `animates\r`
# (which matches nothing). Both `verify/scenes/` and `verify/pr/` use this extension.
#
# ⚠️ **This one is REASONED, NOT MEASURED, and the distinction matters** (organon#658).
# Unlike the shebang case above — which the workstation tested and disproved for Git Bash
# — nobody has watched a `.scene` directive come back with a trailing CR, because
# `verify.sh` exits at its line-136 platform gate before it ever parses a scene on
# Windows. MSYS's CR-stripping is a property of *bash reading its own input*; it says
# nothing about what `sed` does to a data file, so the leniency that rescued the shebangs
# is not expected to apply here. Treat this as a prediction with a mechanism behind it,
# and confirm it once the line-136 gate is fixed and the harness actually runs.
*.scene text eol=lf

# ─── Binary pins ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `text=auto` already detects all of these correctly today (`git ls-files --eol` reports
# them `-text`), so these lines change nothing right now. They are insurance: detection
# is a heuristic over the first 8000 bytes, and a golden frame that got CRLF-mangled on
# checkout would fail `verify.sh` as a *rendering* regression — days of chasing the GPU
# for a line-ending bug. Cheap to state, expensive to discover.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf binary
*.bin binary
*.gguf binary
*.wav binary
*.mp3 binary
*.mp4 binary
*.mov binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary

# The Yale Bright Star Catalog, `include_bytes!`d by `organon-render/src/stars.rs`. The
# only extensionless binary in the tree, so it is the one file where the heuristic is
# working without a net; a mangled byte here decodes into a wrong sky.
native/organon-render/src/BSC5 binary

# ─── SKILL.md: the one Markdown that CRLF breaks (organon#19, measured 2026-08-13) ─────
#
# Claude Code reads a skill's YAML frontmatter from between two `---` fences. Its parser
# does not accept `---\r\n`, so on a Windows working tree the frontmatter silently fails
# to parse and the skill degrades in a way that reports nothing:
#
#   * `name` falls back to the directory name — which is why the skill still appears in
#     `system/init`'s `slash_commands` and looks installed;
#   * `description` falls back to the body's first heading;
#   * and the skill is never offered to the model at all — absent from `skills`.
#
# Nothing errors, nothing warns. From the outside it is indistinguishable from an agent
# that simply chose not to use the skill, which is how `organon-cli` went unread for days
# while its SKILL.md was actively maintained.
#
# MEASURED against a real `claude -p` session, with three controls sitting in the same
# directory. `~/.claude/skills` held four skills; `organon-cli` was the only one with
# CRLF (LF=318, CR=318 — the other three had CR=0) and the only one missing from the
# offered `skills` array. Converting a BYTE-IDENTICAL copy to LF took the offered count
# 22 → 23 and flipped its description from the H1 fallback to the real one. Each of these
# was ruled out first, by experiment rather than by argument: the junction at
# `~/.claude/skills/organon-cli` (a real directory copy fails identically), file size (a
# 22 986-byte sibling loads; this one is 19 924), description length (a 607-char sibling
# loads; this one is 582), a BOM, duplicate copies elsewhere on the machine, a colliding
# slash command, a disabling setting, and the skill's own name.
#
# ⚠️ The index was already LF throughout (`git ls-files --eol` → `i/lf w/crlf`), so this
# is purely a checkout-conversion bug: nothing was ever committed wrong, and `text=auto`
# did exactly what it promises. That is what makes it invisible to review — the file is
# correct in the repository and broken on disk, for Windows checkouts only.
#
# Note this pin's history, because it is the third fix in the same place: the skill was
# originally a git symlink (unusable on a Windows checkout without `core.symlinks=true`),
# then became a real tracked file, which is what gave it a CRLF working copy. Each fix
# uncovered the next failure.
.claude/skills/**/SKILL.md text eol=lf

# ─── CHANGELOG.md: union was not enough, because GitHub ignores it ──────────────────────
#
# ✅ **RESOLVED by `changelog.d/` — new entries no longer go in this file at all.** The
# rest of this block is why, kept because the measurements are what decided it and a bare
# `merge=union` line with no history would look like superstition to whoever reads it next.
#
# **The problem.** The file has one shared insertion point — a `## Unreleased` block that
# every PR adds a section to the top of — so any two open branches conflict there by
# construction, whether or not they touch a single common concern. Measured on 2026-08-13:
# four PRs open at once, four hand-resolutions, each on a tree nobody had compiled, and the
# resolution was "keep both sides" every single time. A conflict whose answer is always the
# same is a merge driver's job, not a person's.
#
# **The first answer, `merge=union`.** It takes both sides of a conflicting hunk in order
# and emits no markers. It is built into git, needs no `merge.*.driver` registration in
# anyone's config, and therefore works for a fresh clone and for CI without a setup step —
# which a custom driver would not. Measured on 2026-08-14: merging `main` into a
# conflicting PR branch locally reported *"Auto-merging CHANGELOG.md … Automatic merge went
# well"*, no conflict, no markers. **It works — for `git`.**
#
# 🚨 **GitHub does not honour it.** GitHub computes PR mergeability with its own three-way
# merge, which ignores `.gitattributes` merge drivers, so the same PR displays
# `CONFLICTING` while `git` resolves it silently. The tax simply moved: someone has to
# merge `main` locally and push, purely to make the PR page agree with what git already
# thinks. On 2026-08-14 that happened on **four separate branches in one day** — the same
# count as the day before, which is the number that ended the argument. Union bought
# nothing at the boundary where the cost is actually paid.
#
# **The answer that holds: `changelog.d/`.** One Markdown fragment per change, named from
# the branch, concatenated into this file at release time by
# `native/tools/changelog.py`. Two branches write two different files, and two different
# files do not conflict — the shared insertion point is gone rather than tolerated. No
# Action, no merge driver, nothing to configure in a fresh clone.
# `changelog.d/README.md` is the contributor-facing half; the tool's module docstring is
# the design.
#
# ⚠️ **The rejected alternative, recorded so it is not re-proposed:** an Action that
# auto-resolves the conflict and pushes to PR branches. Wrong here, because this repo
# routinely has many agent worktrees live at once — it would push to a branch someone is
# mid-commit on.
#
# **`merge=union` STAYS, as a belt, and only for as long as it is one.** `## Unreleased`
# remains in the file permanently as the release step's second input, and PRs opened before
# this change still carry entries written into it. While any of those are in flight, two of
# them can still collide, and union is still the right resolution for that collision.
#
# ⚠️ **It is right for THIS file because of how this file is written, not in general.**
# Union is correct when both sides *add* distinct blocks. If two branches edit the *same
# existing line* — rewording one entry two ways — union silently keeps **both copies**,
# with no conflict to warn anyone. That is a worse failure than a conflict, and it is why
# this is scoped to one path rather than applied to Markdown at large. Do not reword a
# landed entry on a branch.
#
# ⚠️ It never made a conflicted PR mergeable on its own. The rebase that prompted it
# conflicted in *two* files, and resolving the changelog half would not have unblocked it.
#
# 📌 `changelog.d/**` needs no attribute of its own, and that is the point rather than an
# omission: one branch owns one fragment file, so there is no hunk for a driver to resolve.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
# The legibility fixtures are byte-pinned test inputs: a test edits them by exact
# substring and a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true turned `…\n` into `…\r\n`,
# so the edit silently did nothing and the test failed only on that machine.
native/organon-render/tests/fixtures/*.txt text eol=lf
